\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A refined cell keeps the factor 3/5}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Fix #{n\ge 0} and a weight #{w>0}. Form the 9 edges in
the refinement of one #{n}-cell of the
\vocabk{gasket graph}{fdip-00DP}, each of weight #{w}. The
\vocabk{kept form}{fdip-00DF} on the three corners equals
#{\frac{3}{5}} times the energy of those corners joined by
three edges of weight #{w}. The eliminated block has
determinant #{50 w^3}. On #{G_{n+1}} itself, with
every edge of weight #{w}, the kept form on #{V_n} equals
#{\frac{3}{5}} times the energy of #{G_n} with every edge of
weight #{w}.}

\proof{
\p{The refinement of any #{n}-cell is the same 9-edge graph
as the refinement of #{G_0}, so the unit-weight calculation
scales by #{w}. Use the labels of the definition.
Declare the ordered counting basis #{(a,b,c)} of midpoint
indicators, in that order. Each midpoint meets two corners
and the other two midpoints, so in that basis the block on
#{(a,b,c)} is #{M=5I-J}, where #{J} is the #{3\times 3}
matrix of ones.
The diagonal entries of #{M} are #{4} and the off-diagonal
entries are #{-1}. Since #{J^2=3J},
##{\Bigl(5I-J\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac{1}{5}I+\frac{1}{10}J\Bigr)
=I+\frac{1}{2}J-\frac{1}{5}J-\frac{1}{10}J^2
=I+\Bigl(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{5}-\frac{3}{10}\Bigr)J.}
The coefficient equals
#{\frac{5}{10}-\frac{2}{10}-\frac{3}{10}=0}, so the product
is #{I} and the second factor is #{M^{-1}}. The determinant
expands as
##{4\bigl(16-1\bigr)-(-1)\bigl(-4-1\bigr)+(-1)\bigl(1+4\bigr)
=60-5-5=50.}
For weight #{w} the block is #{wM}, with determinant
#{50 w^3}.}

\p{The neighbors of #{a} are #{B}, #{C}, #{b}, and #{c}, so
the Laplacian vanishes at #{a} when #{4a=B+C+b+c}. The same
count at #{b} and at #{c} gives the other two rows of #{M}.
For a column #{v},
##{M^{-1}v=\frac{1}{5}v+\frac{1}{10}\Bigl(\sum_i v_i\Bigr)(1,1,1).}
The first right-hand entry is #{B+C} and the three entries
sum to #{2(A+B+C)}, so the first output entry is
#{\frac{B+C}{5}+\frac{A+B+C}{5}}. Hence
##{a=\frac{A+2B+2C}{5},\quad
b=\frac{2A+B+2C}{5},\quad
c=\frac{2A+2B+C}{5},}
where the formulas for #{b} and #{c} are the same calculation
with the corners cycled. For #{A=1}, #{B=0}, and #{C=0}, the
midpoint values are #{\frac{1}{5}}, #{\frac{2}{5}}, and
#{\frac{2}{5}}. In the edge order of \ref{fdip-00DR}, the
squared differences are
##{\frac{9}{25},\frac{9}{25},0,\frac{1}{25},\frac{4}{25},
\frac{1}{25},\frac{1}{25},\frac{4}{25},\frac{1}{25}.}
Their sum is #{\frac{6}{5}}. The block is positive definite,
so \ref{fdip-00DH} identifies this extension as the
minimizer.}

\p{Declare the ordered counting basis of the three corner
indicators. Every permutation of the corners extends to an
automorphism by the same permutation of the opposite
midpoints, so the three off-diagonal entries of the kept
form are equal. Constants have energy #{0} and lie in the
kernel. In that basis, a symmetric #{3\times 3} matrix with
those two properties is a multiple
of the unit-triangle Laplacian. At #{(1,0,0)} that triangle
has energy #{2}, since two edges contribute #{1} and the
third contributes #{0}. The multiple is
#{\frac{6}{5}\cdot\frac{1}{2}=\frac{3}{5}}.
Multiplying every edge weight by #{w} multiplies both
quadratic forms by #{w}.}

\p{On #{G_{n+1}}, \ref{fdip-00DR} puts the midpoints of distinct
#{n}-cells in disjoint sets and partitions the edges into the
cells. Each midpoint is joined to a corner of its own cell.
\ref{fdip-00DJ} adds the cell minima. Each minimum is
#{\frac{3}{5}} times the energy of that cell's three boundary
edges of weight #{w}, and those boundary edges are the edges
of #{G_n}.}
}
